#!/bin/sh
# git-stash-guard: reference-transaction hook
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   This repo runs 60+ concurrent agent worktrees against one shared .git
#   directory. `git stash` is repo-global: the stash stack (refs/stash and
#   its reflog) is shared across every worktree, so one session's
#   `git stash pop` can steal another session's entry. Agents are told not
#   to use `git stash` in their briefs, but "asking" does not hold at this
#   concurrency -- an agent used it anyway on 2026-07-28 and got lucky.
#   This hook makes the highest-value part of that mistake -- creating a
#   NEW shared stash entry -- fail loudly instead of silently succeeding.
#
# WHAT THIS ACTUALLY BLOCKS (verified empirically, see docs/evidence or the
#   PR that introduced this file for the throwaway-repo transcript):
#   - `git stash`, `git stash push`, `git stash save`   -> ALWAYS blocked.
#   - `git stash clear`                                  -> ALWAYS blocked.
#   - `git stash drop <last remaining entry>`            -> blocked (this is
#     the case where dropping requires deleting refs/stash entirely).
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT BLOCK (known, tested gap -- do not assume otherwise):
#   - `git stash apply` never touches a ref at all, so no hook of any kind
#     (this one included) ever fires for it. It cannot be blocked this way.
#   - `git stash pop` / `git stash drop <entry>` when the stack has more
#     than one entry left afterward: git rewrites the reflog and repoints
#     refs/stash WITHOUT going through the transactional ref-update API,
#     so this hook is never invoked for that case either. With 80+ existing
#     entries in this repo's stack, this is the common case, not the edge
#     case -- treat `pop`/`drop`/`apply` of EXISTING entries as still
#     unenforced. The standing prohibition on touching `git stash` at all
#     remains a policy rule for those subcommands, not a technical one.
#
# BYPASS (for a human who knows what they're doing, in a clean, single-
#   worktree context -- not the concurrent-agent failure mode this guards
#   against):
#     ALLOW_GIT_STASH=1 git stash push -m "..."
#
# SAFE ALTERNATIVE (what to use instead, since the underlying need --
#   comparing with/without your changes -- is real):
#   - `git worktree add ../scratch-<name> -b scratch/<name>` and copy the
#     change over, or
#   - a scratch branch: `git branch wip/<name> && git commit -am wip`, or
#   - `git diff > /tmp/patch.diff` (or your scratchpad dir) and `git apply`
#     it back later.
#
# Git only aborts a ref-transaction when this hook is invoked with the
# "prepared" state and exits non-zero; "committed"/"aborted" invocations
# are notifications only and cannot change the outcome, so this hook is a
# no-op for those states.
state="$1"
if [ "$state" != "prepared" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

if [ -n "$ALLOW_GIT_STASH" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

# ROUTINE MAINTENANCE CARVE-OUT (added 2026-07-29 after a live false positive)
#
# `git pack-refs` moves refs/stash from loose to packed. Empirically -- measured
# in a throwaway repo, not assumed -- it emits TWO refs/stash transactions:
#
#   git pack-refs --all   0000000 -> 96a68b4   (indistinguishable from a create)
#   git pack-refs --all   96a68b4 -> 0000000   (indistinguishable from a clear)
#
# Compare a real stash create (0000000 -> 96a68b4) and `git stash clear`
# (96a68b4 -> 0000000). The old/new OIDs are IDENTICAL in shape, so NO
# OID-based rule can separate maintenance from a real stash operation. An
# earlier attempt at "allow when old == new" was wrong for exactly this reason.
#
# This bit us for real: `git commit` triggered git's auto-gc, which ran
# pack-refs, which this hook aborted --
#     fatal: ref updates aborted by hook / failed to run pack-refs / task 'gc' failed
# The commit itself survived, but this fires on EVERY auto-gc, and a guard that
# breaks routine maintenance is a guard someone eventually disables.
#
# So the discriminator is the invoking git subcommand, read from the parent
# process. This is deliberately a *cooperative* control, not a security
# boundary: it exists to stop accidental `git stash` among cooperating agent
# sessions, and $PPID args are trivially spoofable by anyone who wants to. That
# is an acceptable trade -- the threat model is a mistake, not an adversary.
#
# FAILS CLOSED: if `ps` is missing or returns nothing, we do NOT take the
# carve-out and fall through to the normal blocking logic.
_parent_cmd=$(ps -o args= -p "$PPID" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
if [ -n "$_parent_cmd" ] && printf '%s' "$_parent_cmd" |
     grep -Eq '(^|[[:space:]])(pack-refs|gc|maintenance)([[:space:]]|$)'; then
  exit 0
fi

blocked=0
while read -r old new refname; do
  case "$refname" in
    refs/stash)
      blocked=1
      ;;
  esac
done

if [ "$blocked" = "1" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
git-stash-guard: BLOCKED a refs/stash update.

`git stash` is disabled in this repo: the stash stack is shared across all
60+ concurrent agent worktrees on this .git directory, and a mis-timed
`git stash pop` can steal another session's entry (it has happened).

Use one of these instead:
  git worktree add ../scratch-<name> -b scratch/<name>   # isolated copy
  git branch wip/<name> && git commit -am wip             # scratch branch
  git diff > /tmp/patch.diff                               # patch file

If you are a human, working alone, in a clean single-worktree context, and
you are sure this is not the shared-stack failure mode above, bypass with:
  ALLOW_GIT_STASH=1 git stash ...

See AGENTS.md ("Git Stash Guard") for the full writeup, including the
subcommands this hook cannot block (`apply`, and `pop`/`drop` of an entry
that isn't the last one in the stack).
EOF
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
